WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.280 --> 00:00:11.040
As americans buy millions of new smartphones&nbsp;
so too do we throw old models into the garbage.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:11.040 --> 00:00:16.640
With them may go old messages but also all sorts&nbsp;
of valuable materials used in the phones. Rare&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:16.640 --> 00:00:21.920
earth minerals. At the same time we dutifully&nbsp;
separate glass and paper in our blue recycling&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:21.920 --> 00:00:26.560
bins despite the fact that much of what we&nbsp;
put out at the curbside actually has little&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:26.560 --> 00:00:33.280
resale or reuse value and it costs our local&nbsp;
government scarce funds to collect. Here's why:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:33.280 --> 00:00:40.080
In 2017 China, once the most common destination,&nbsp;
closed its doors to foreign recyclables. Cities&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:40.080 --> 00:00:44.960
must pay now to have recyclables taken&nbsp;
off their hands. And the contents of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:44.960 --> 00:00:50.480
those virtuous blue bins — often negative.&nbsp;
That's why many cities have halted recycling&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:51.120 --> 00:00:58.160
altogether. We need to rethink recycling as a&nbsp;
sort of trash mine. Separate the valuable stuff&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:58.160 --> 00:01:04.160
and stop spending scarce post-pandemic public&nbsp;
dollars to collect stuff we actually have to pay&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:04.160 --> 00:01:10.080
to get rid of. Let's get smart about recycling.&nbsp;
Do you think recycling still makes sense?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:10.800 --> 00:01:16.080
Let us know in our poll. Also let us know what&nbsp;
other topics you want our scholars to cover&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:16.080 --> 00:01:22.800
in 60 seconds and be sure to like and subscribe&nbsp;
for more research and videos from AEI.

